bols used on the main site plan (heritage trees, trees to remain, trees to be removed, and protection fencing). READ the block content to understand the symbol definitions, then use VISION to cross-reference these symbols on the Block 1 drawing.\n    30→\n    31→*   **Block 3: notes — Special Construction Techniques**\n    32→    Outlines five specific techniques for protecting tree root zones during excavation, including root cutting, mulch requirements, and vapor barriers. READ the block content for exact excavation and root protection specifications required by the City Arborist. \n    33→\n    34→*   **Block 2: notes — Remedial Tree Care Notes**\n    35→    Details a six-point program for aeration and supplemental nutrients for trees impacted by construction areas. READ the block content for specific soil/foliar analysis rules, fertilization composition guidelines, and pre/post-construction treatment protocols.\n    36→\n    37→*   **Blocks 4 & 6: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**\n    38→    Contains the title block, landscape architect information (Blu Fish Collaborative), project location details, professional seal, sheet number (27 of 35), north arrow, and scale bar. READ for general project administrative details and use VISION to verify drawing orientation and scale.\n    39→\n    40→---\n    41→\n    42→- `block-3.md` — notes: This section provides detailed notes on remedial tree care, specifically focusing on aeration and su\n    43→\n    44→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    45→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85cce1e8-6e77-4208-9a6d-06fe1aa951a1","uuid":"4b51bd80-af67-44ef-ad79-b4b53b3