{"level":30,"time":1778538226408,"pid":99088,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018sbuR3tBYFGEvYus4TYQAF","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 17 — Conveyance Cross Sections\n     2→\n     3→Conveyance cross-section graphs and corresponding site plan view.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet contains the conveyance cross-sections and a corresponding site plan snippet for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to model and display the hydraulic performance (water surface elevations, velocities, and channel depths) of the site's drainage conveyance system during 25-year and 100-year storm events at three distinct cross-section locations (XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3).\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   **Manning's Roughness Coefficient (n):** 0.03 used for all cross-sections\n    11→*   **Design Flow Rates (Q):** 16.49 cfs (25-Year event), 21.97 cfs (100-Year event) across all sections\n    12→*   **Maximum 100-Year Velocity:** 8.70 ft/s (at cross-section XS 3)\n    13→*   **Maximum 100-Year Depth:** 0.48 ft (at cross-section XS 3)\n    14→*   **Benchmark 1:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n    15→*   **Benchmark 2:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n    16→*   **Finished Floor Elevations (FFE):** Bldg. 5 = 683.0 ft, Bldg. 6 = 683.0 ft\n    17→\n    18→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    19→\n    20→*   **Block 7: site plan — Multi-family development plan view**\n    21→    Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, grading contours, drainage easements, and the specific locations of the cross-section cut lines (XS 1, XS 2, XS 3) relative to buildings 5 and 6 and existing retaining walls.\n    22→*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6: diagrams — Cross-section profile graphs**\n    23→    Use VISION to examine the channel profiles, side slopes, and plotted water surface elevations for cross-sections XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3 under both 25-year and 100-year storm conditions. \n    24→*   **Blocks 14, 15, 16, 17, 19, 20: tables — Hydraulic data tables**\n    25→    READ for exact hydraulic parameters corresponding to each cross-section graph, including Depth (ft), Flow Rate Q (cfs), Area (sqft), Velocity (ft/s), Wetted Perimeter Wp (ft), Critical Depth Yc (ft), Top Width (ft), and Energy Head (ft).\n    26→*   **Block 21: text_block — Hydraulic modeling note**\n    27→    READ to verify the Manning's roughness coefficient used in the calculations.\n    28→*   **Block 12: notes — Benchmarks**\n    29→    READ for exact elevation values and physical descriptions of the survey benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2).\n    30→*   **Block 8: notes — Legend**\n    31→    READ for definitions of line types and symbols used in the plan view, such as existing/proposed contours, storm drain lines, and limits of construction.\n    32→*   **Block 9: notes — Construction and landscape notes**\n    33→    READ for curb construction grading instructions (catch curb vs. spill curb) and references to the tree list.\n    34→*   **Blocks 10, 11, 13, 18: various — Boilerplate, Title Block & Administrative**\n    35→    READ for the legal description of the property, project title (Valley View Townhomes), engineer seal (Bleyl Engineering), standard utility/Texas 811 warnings, and city submittal disclaimers.\n    36→\n    37→---\n    38→\n    39→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    40→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cf9b9a6-a069-491a-8521-ba77a83a73f4","uuid":"857ef53e-e4ee-4b1e-922a-119091eca3a2","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-17/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 17 — Conveyance Cross Sections\n\nConveyance cross-section graphs and corresponding site plan view.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet contains the conveyance cross-sections and a corresponding site plan snippet for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to model and display the hydraulic performance (water surface elevations, velocities, and channel depths) of the site's drainage conveyance system during 25-year and 100-year storm events at three distinct cross-section locations (XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3).\n\n**Key values:**\n*   **Manning's Roughness Coefficient (n):** 0.03 used for all cross-sections\n*   **Design Flow Rates (Q):** 16.49 cfs (25-Year event), 21.97 cfs (100-Year event) across all sections\n*   **Maximum 100-Year Velocity:** 8.70 ft/s (at cross-section XS 3)\n*   **Maximum 100-Year Depth:** 0.48 ft (at cross-section XS 3)\n*   **Benchmark 1:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n*   **Benchmark 2:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n*   **Finished Floor Elevations (FFE):** Bldg. 5 = 683.0 ft, Bldg. 6 = 683.0 ft\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Block 7: site plan — Multi-family development plan view**\n    Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, grading contours, drainage easements, and the specific locations of the cross-section cut lines (XS 1, XS 2, XS 3) relative to buildings 5 and 6 and existing retaining walls.\n*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6: diagrams — Cross-section profile graphs**\n    Use VISION to examine the channel profiles, side slopes, and plotted water surface elevations for cross-sections XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3 under both 25-year and 100-year storm conditions. \n*   **Blocks 14, 15, 16, 17, 19, 20: tables — Hydraulic data tables**\n    READ for exact hydraulic parameters corresponding to each cross-section graph, including Depth (ft), Flow Rate Q (cfs), Area (sqft), Velocity (ft/s), Wetted Perimeter Wp (ft), Critical Depth Yc (ft), Top Width (ft), and Energy Head (ft).\n*   **Block 21: text_block — Hydraulic modeling note**\n    READ to verify the Manning's roughness coefficient used in the calculations.\n*   **Block 12: notes — Benchmarks**\n    READ for exact elevation values and physical descriptions of the survey benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2).\n*   **Block 8: notes — Legend**\n    READ for definitions of line types and symbols used in the plan view, such as existing/proposed contours, storm drain lines, and limits of construction.\n*   **Block 9: notes — Construction and landscape notes**\n    READ for curb construction grading instructions (catch curb vs. spill curb) and references to the tree list.\n*   **Blocks 10, 11, 13, 18: various — Boilerplate, Title Block & Administrative**\n    READ for the legal description of the property, project title (Valley View Townhomes), engineer seal (Bleyl Engineering), standard utility/Texas 811 warnings, and city submittal disclaimers.\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":40,"startLine":1,"totalLines":40}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778538226408,"pid":99088,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01QfstpGuTjjY3FHfZFQ5yyC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Conveyance Cross Sections (Sheet 17) are for drainage only, not for overhead electric clearances. The Utility Tap Profile (Sheet 20) is for water line connections and doesn't show overhead electric line clearances either.\n\nNow let me systematically evaluate all the checklist items based on what I've found:\n\n**Key Findings from Research:**\n1. **Overhead Electric Line Present**: An overhead electric line (OHE) is shown on the Landscape Plan (Sheet 31) running along the southern boundary of the site\n2. **Trees Near Overhead Line**: Vision check confirmed multiple trees positioned within 10 feet of the OHE line\n3. **Tree Species Issue**: The plant list includes:\n   - Live Oak (2) - NOT utility-compatible, grows >40 ft\n   - Cedar Elm (10) - NOT utility-compatible, grows 40-50 ft\n   - Texas Mountain Laurel (2) - Utility-compatible (small tree)\n   - Mexican Redbud (6) - Utility-compatible (small tree)\n4. **No Clearance Dimensions**: No explicit clearance dimensions or measurements are shown between the OHE line and any site features (buildings, driveways, fences)\n5. **No Profile/Elevation Views**: No building elevation sheets, section views, or profile exhibits showing vertical clearances to the OHE line\n6. **No 48-hour Notification Note**: Need to check if there's a note on the cover sheet about Austin Energy notification\n\nNow let me go through each checklist item and determine its status:\n\n**Applicability Assessment:**\n- This project has 8 buildings, driveways, parking areas, retaining walls, landscape with trees, and an overhead electric line along the southern boundary\n- The overhead electric line is shown on the Landscape Plan but not clearly marked on other sheets\n- No transmission lines (voltage ≥60kV) are mentioned\n- Landscape plan clearly shows OHE, so this grouping APPLIES\n\n\n- For EL-1.1, buildings are present and the OHE exists, but no clearance dimensions or conductor elevation data are provided, making verification impossible. For EL-1.2, customer facilities like buildings and parking areas are shown, yet conductor type and radial clearance measurements aren't identified anywhere in the plans. EL-1.3 involves building projections, which I need to assess next.\n\nBuilding projections likely appear on the plans, but without elevation or profile views showing their relationship to overhead equipment, I cannot verify compliance. Gas pump canopies don't apply since this is residential development. Elevated structures like stairs or walkways may be proposed, though I'd need more detailed architectural information to confirm. The property's proximity to Valley View Road means adjacent building requirements could apply, but no clearance verification is documented. Conductor elevations have been surveyed for the overhead lines serving the site, yet this data isn't referenced in the submitted materials.\n\nSection and plan drawings showing actual clearance measurements between outer conductors and proposed structures are missing entirely. Perimeter fencing appears on the site plan, but without dimensional callouts or clear positioning relative to the overhead equipment line, I can't verify the required 7.5-foot separation. Similarly, fence post locations relative to existing or proposed electric poles aren't clearly marked, making the 10-foot clearance requirement impossible to confirm. Substation-related fencing requirements don't apply here.\n\nFor retaining walls shown on Sheet 38, the alignment and clearance from the overhead line conductors aren't documented with sections or dimensions. The proposed driveways need to maintain adequate vertical clearance to the conductors as well.\n\nParking areas are included in the site plan but lack verification of vertical clearance dimensions. Existing electric poles may conflict with the proposed driveway layout, though their exact locations and any required relocation aren't clearly indicated. Clearance measurements need to be taken directly from the conductor positions to verify compliance with all applicable standards.\n\nStreetlight placement requires confirmation through the lighting plan to determine if any fall beneath overhead lines. Sidewalk routing at property boundaries isn't sufficiently detailed to confirm adequate separation from utility poles. Trash enclosure locations should be verified against the overhead line positions. Customer-owned facilities shown on the site plan need evaluation for potential conflicts with distribution infrastructure.\n\nTrees with established canopies positioned over the overhead line represent a clear violation with no documented analysis provided. Water meter placement must be checked against utility drawings to ensure the required 7.5-foot clearance from service drops. Water line paths are indicated but lack dimensioned clearances from distribution equipment. Building foundations positioned near property lines don't show specific setback dimensions needed to verify clearance compliance. Vertical clearance measurements from mid-span locations require surveyor documentation to confirm adequate spacing.\n\nNo registered surveyor data has been submitted to validate mid-span clearances. Overhead line crossings at perpendicular angles don't apply since no new lines cross roadways. Secondary line positioning over primary lines is unclear and marked not applicable. Marquee signage isn't part of this proposal. The conversion to underground service means no new poles are being installed. Decorative lighting placement near the overhead line needs verification on the plans. The proposed Live Oak and Cedar Elm trees are incompatible species for proximity to overhead lines and fail the utility-compatibility requirement.\n\nElectric meters have been proposed and their locations require confirmation against Sheet 21. Existing streetlights in the area may need clearance verification. Down guy wires from distribution poles could potentially conflict with accessible paths and driveways, but their exact locations and any conflicts haven't been documented. Guy easements for transmission anchors don't apply here since this is distribution-level infrastructure. Down guy anchor locations and any required easements for angled supports remain unverified and undocumented.\n\nPad-mounted transformers shown on Sheet 21 need to maintain 7.5 feet of vertical clearance to overhead lines, though this clearance hasn't been confirmed. Niche-mounted transformer configurations don't apply since all units are pad-mounted. Construction staging activities will occur near energized lines and require a 10-foot radial clearance per OSHA standards, but the construction logistics plan hasn't been provided to verify this requirement. A 48-hour notification note for work near overhead lines needs to be confirmed on the cover sheet.\n\nService drops passing through roofs require clearance verification, but the service-mast installations aren't detailed on the building elevations. I should review the General Notes and cover sheet to confirm whether the 48-hour notification requirement is documented.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2658,"cache_read_input_tokens":67272,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2658},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cf9b9a6-a069-491a-8521-ba77a83a73f4","uuid":"9e324331-a071-409a-b569-65057c2a8020"}
