{"level":30,"time":1778542921069,"pid":99088,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018STZVHcbMzgkKXyVf7tziR","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 19\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n     4→\n     5→Electric Notes\n     6→1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     7→2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     8→3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n     9→4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n    10→5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n    11→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8aec104c-0835-4d66-891b-4f34e6fe876f","uuid":"fb99a567-92a5-4a04-b9f5-0141e9068bc8","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-19.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 19\n\n## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n\nElectric Notes\n1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n","numLines":11,"startLine":1,"totalLines":11}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778542921069,"pid":99088,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01GsTE3bWrQHrpAingEpg5UC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"CRITICAL FINDINGS:\n\nBlock 13 (Electric Transmission Notes) - NOTE 1 states:\n\"No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure.\"\n\nBlock 17 & 19 (Electric Notes) state:\n\"The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment.\"\n\nThese notes mention transmission facilities, but based on the vision checks, the OHE line shown on the landscape plan appears to be a distribution line (not transmission), as there was no mention of \"transmission\" labeling on the landscape plan itself.\n\nHowever, the key insight is:\n- The site plan notes (Block 13 Note 1) state \"No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the transmission structure\"\n- But this applies to TRANSMISSION facilities specifically\n\nNow I need to determine:\n1. Are the overhead electric lines shown on the landscape plan DISTRIBUTION or TRANSMISSION lines?\n2. If DISTRIBUTION: The UCM § 1.10.10.4 limits are:\n   - 15 ft for non-utility-compatible trees\n   - 25 ft for large trees (40+ ft)\n3. If TRANSMISSION: The UCM § 1.10.10.4 limits are:\n   - 50 ft for non-utility-compatible trees\n   - 50 ft for large trees\n\nBased on the vision checks:\n- Trees are 0.1 to 2 feet from the OHE line\n- These are clearly large trees (Cedar Elm and Live Oak based on the plant list)\n- No distinction is made on the landscape plan between distribution and transmission\n\nFrom the facts.md, I saw:\n\"5' Electric Easements (×2)\" and other small easement widths, but no transmission easement mentioned there. \n\nThe transmission notes on Sheet 3 may reference a separate easement area, though the failed vision check for EL-2.5 prevented full analysis. The plat on Sheet 2 should clarify whether transmission easements exist on this site. Regardless of line type, trees positioned 0-2 feet from the OHE line violate clearance requirements under either distribution or transmission standards—they'd need to be utility-compatible within 15 feet for distribution, or entirely outside 50 feet for transmission.\n\nChecking the plant schedule against clearance rules, the Cedar Elm (10 trees, 40-50+ feet mature) and Live Oak (2 trees, 40-80 feet) are both non-utility-compatible species. The Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud also don't appear on utility-compatible exception lists. Since the landscape plan marks trees near the OHE line as \"M\" (Mitigation Trees) and the schedule identifies Cedar Elm and Live Oak as the primary large shade trees, these mitigation plantings are almost certainly non-compliant species positioned too close to the overhead line.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2483,"cache_read_input_tokens":70678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2483},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8aec104c-0835-4d66-891b-4f34e6fe876f","uuid":"b9e5215d-08c3-4f18-bf51-20abea39ae67"}
{"level":30,"time":1778542921069,"pid":99088,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01GsTE3bWrQHrpAingEpg5UC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my comprehensive research, let me now compile the findings. Let me verify one more critical detail about which trees are shown near the OHE line to ensure accurate species identification."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2483,"cache_read_input_tokens":70678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2483},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8aec104c-0835-4d66-891b-4f34e6fe876f","uuid":"adf4d6e5-78af-4ee2-9a45-28a9a84235db"}
{"level":30,"time":1778542921069,"pid":99088,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01GsTE3bWrQHrpAingEpg5UC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AkzUVZq2enGUDZppR2BQ7k","name":"mcp__conductor_tools__vision_check","input":{"checklistItemId":"el-md-exp:EL-2.1","checklistItemText":"Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment","question":"On Sheet 31, look at the plant legend symbols. Can you identify what the circles/symbols mean for the trees shown at the bottom edge near the OHE line? Based on the legend, these are marked 'M' for Mitigation Trees. According to the plant schedule on Sheet 35, the proposed trees are Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Texas Mountain Laurel, and Mexican Redbud. Given their typical mature heights (Cedar Elm 40-50 ft, Live Oak 40-80 ft, Texas Mountain Laurel 25 ft, Mexican Redbud 20-30 ft), which of these would NOT be utility-compatible (i.e., which exceed 20 feet mature height except the noted exceptions)?","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2483,"cache_read_input_tokens":70678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2483},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8aec104c-0835-4d66-891b-4f34e6fe876f","uuid":"71778c0c-94c5-42e9-9fe2-8907717e649d"}
